`phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Counter", Counter, .{})` binds a stub class to a Zig implementation and returns a wrapper type for `module.classes`.

Methods can use typed parameters and returns or parse arguments manually through a call context. See [Argument parsing and return values]($link.page('reference/registration/arguments')).

# [Zig instance data]($heading.id('zig-backing'))

A Zig struct with fields adds per-instance data to each PHP object. If every field has a default, phpz initializes this data automatically. Otherwise initialize it in the constructor or `.init`. Calling a method that requires `self` on an uninitialized instance raises a PHP error.

If `.init` returns an error, the instance data remains uninitialized. phpz marks construction as failed: `new` skips `__construct` and the object skips `__destruct`. The exception stays pending. Object destruction cleans up only initialized instance data.

## [Method bindings]($heading.id('method-bindings'))

In automatic mode, each `pub fn` corresponds to a concrete method in the stub:

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;
class Counter {
    public function __construct(int $n = 0) {}
    public static function isPositiveStep(int $n): bool {}
    public function add(int $n): void {}
    public function value(): int {}
}
```

```zig
const Counter = struct {
    n: i64 = 0,

    pub fn __construct(n: ?i64) Counter {
        return .{ .n = n orelse 0 };
    }

    pub fn isPositiveStep(n: i64) bool {
        return n > 0;
    }

    pub fn add(self: *Counter, n: i64) void {
        self.n +|= n;
    }

    pub fn value(self: *const Counter) i64 {
        return self.n;
    }
};

const CounterClass = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Counter", Counter, .{});
```

```php
use MyPHPExt\Counter;

var_dump(Counter::isPositiveStep(3)); // bool(true)
$counter = new Counter(5);
$counter->add(3);
echo $counter->value();               // 8
```

| PHP call | Zig parameters | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `new Counter(5)` | `n: ?i64` | The constructor returns a `Counter` to initialize the object's Zig data. PHP receives the object. |
| `Counter::isPositiveStep(3)` | `n: i64` | A static method takes only the supplied integer and returns a PHP boolean. |
| `$counter->add(3)` | `self: *Counter, n: i64` | phpz supplies the receiver from `$counter`. The method updates that instance and returns `void`. |
| `$counter->value()` | `self: *const Counter` | PHP supplies no arguments. Zig reads the receiver and returns a PHP integer. |

An ordinary instance method on a class with Zig fields **must** take `*T` or `*const T` first. A constructor for that class takes no receiver and returns `T` or `!T`. PHP declares no constructor return type, so reflection returns `null` from `getReturnType()`. A static method also takes no receiver. When a handler needs `Ctx` or `GuardCtx`, put it before the PHP arguments and after any receiver. For classes that keep their state in PHP properties, see [Standard layout method bindings]($link.ref('standard-method-bindings')).

Class and method names must match the stub exactly, including case. Automatic binding checks all public Zig functions and all concrete stub methods. Explicit `@alias` entries share the implementation. At compile time phpz checks parameter counts, optional and variadic parameters, and reference flags against arginfo. A backed constructor skips the PHP return-reference check. Other methods follow the function rules. Match types and defaults in the stub and implementation.

Classes with Zig instance data cannot extend a class that allocates its own objects, such as `Exception`, `ArrayObject` or another class with Zig data. Zend preserves the parent's `create_object` and parent methods expect its layout. Debug builds warn and fail registration with `error.UnsupportedParent`. For these subclasses use `void` as the Zig type and keep state in PHP properties.

Use `.register` when a generated registration function needs a parent or interfaces. The adapter takes a tuple of `*phpz.ClassEntry` values in stub parameter order. Pass each wrapper's `.entry`. Return `*ClassEntry` or an error union containing it. See [Registering parent classes and interfaces]($link.page('reference/registration/declarations').ref('inheritance')).

Use `.methods` to map different names. The mapping must cover every concrete method. For example, rename Zig's `add` to `increment` while keeping the PHP name `add`:

```zig
const CounterClass = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Counter", Counter, .{
    .methods = .{
        .__construct = Counter.__construct,
        .isPositiveStep = Counter.isPositiveStep,
        .add = Counter.increment,
        .value = Counter.value,
    },
});
```

Keep helpers for `.init`, `.deinit`, `.clone` and `.gc` private to exclude them from automatic method binding. See [Object lifetime and garbage collection]($link.page('advanced/objects')) for resource and clone rules.

# [Creating and accessing Zig instance data]($heading.id('backing-access'))

After class registration, the backed `CounterClass` provides these operations:

| API | Behavior and ownership |
| --- | --- |
| `create()` | Create an object and run `.init`, skipping the PHP constructor. Returns an owned wrapper |
| `new(params, named_params)` | Create an object and call the PHP constructor. Returns an owned wrapper |
| `tryNew(params, named_params)` | Like `new`, but catches a Zend bailout as `error.ZendBailout` |
| `fromObject(object)` / `fromZval(value)` | Check the class and handlers, then borrow the wrapper without incrementing the reference count. `fromZval` follows PHP references |
| `backing()` | Borrow a `?*Counter`, or return `null` for uninitialized data |
| `object()` | Borrow a `*zend.Object` |

These two global functions reuse `CounterClass`. Declare them in the stub first. If they are the only global functions, use `phpz.functions(functions, .{ .namespace = "MyPHPExt" })`. Otherwise include them in the `namedFunctions` mapping that covers all global functions:

```php
namespace MyPHPExt;
function makeCounter(int $n): Counter {}
function counterValue(Counter $counter): int {}
```

```zig
const functions = struct {
    pub fn makeCounter(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
        const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{.{ .int = .{} }}, {});
        const number = phpz.Zval.raw.init(.int, args[0]);
        const instance = try CounterClass.new(.{number}, null);
        ctx.ret(.object, instance.object());
    }

    pub fn counterValue(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
        const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
            .{ .object = .{ .instanceof = true } },
        }, .{.{ .class = CounterClass.entry }});
        const instance = try CounterClass.fromObject(args[0]);
        const data = instance.backing() orelse return error.UninitializedCounter;
        ctx.ret(.int, data.n);
    }
};
```

`makeCounter` transfers one object reference to the PHP return value. If only Zig uses the new instance, call `instance.object().release()` during normal cleanup. `fromObject` borrows its result. Do not release the input object on that account or use `backing()` after the object is released.

```php
$counter = MyPHPExt\makeCounter(5);
echo MyPHPExt\counterValue($counter), PHP_EOL; // 5
```

`fromObject` accepts subclasses with compatible handlers. It returns `InvalidObjectLayout` for a related class with an incompatible layout and leaves initialization to the caller. Constructor exceptions return `PhpException` and internal exits return `UnwindExit`. `tryNew` also catches an escaping `ZendBailout`. End the request after `UnwindExit` or `ZendBailout`, as described in [Bailouts and cleanup]($link.page('advanced/bailout')).

# [Standard layout]($heading.id('standard-layout'))

Use `void` or a Zig struct with no fields for Zend's standard object layout. Store instance state in PHP properties declared in the stub.

## [Standard method bindings]($heading.id('standard-method-bindings'))

With the standard layout, methods take no `self`. Retrieve the object with `ctx.call.this()` to access PHP properties:

```zig
const Counter = struct {
    pub fn __construct(ctx: phpz.Ctx, n: ?i64) !void {
        try ctx.call.this().?.setProperty(.int, "n", n orelse 0);
    }

    pub fn value(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
        try ctx.call.expectNoArgs();
        const object = ctx.call.this().?;

        var scratch = phpz.Zval.raw.undef;
        defer phpz.Zval.raw.release(&scratch);

        const n = try object.property("n", false, &scratch);
        ctx.ret(.int, try n.as(.int));
    }
};

const CounterClass = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Counter", Counter, .{});
```

Declare PHP properties in the stub. Zig struct fields alone do not expose public PHP properties.

# [Properties]($heading.id('properties'))

Declare the PHP property in the stub:

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;
class Counter {
    public int $n = 0;
    public ?string $name = null;

    public function __construct(int $n = 0) {}
    public function value(): int {}
}
```

In an instance method, retrieve the object with `ctx.call.this()` and use `property` or `setProperty`. Property access may execute user code, enforce visibility and type constraints, or raise a PHP exception.
